For real numbers $a < b$:

- $a + c < b + c$ and $a - c < b - c$ (the sign is kept);
- $ac < bc$ and $\dfrac{a}{c} < \dfrac{b}{c}$ if $c > 0$ (kept);
- $ac > bc$ and $\dfrac{a}{c} > \dfrac{b}{c}$ if $c < 0$ (**reversed**).

Example from $2 < 5$: multiplying by $-3$ gives $-6 > -15$. The same rules hold for $\le$ and $\ge$. Taking reciprocals of positive numbers also reverses a sign.
